// src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/alloc_wal.pdx -- R52.M3-002 (#1693)
//
// PDXFS-ON-BLOCK v1 -- BITMAP_SET / BITMAP_CLEAR WAL RECORD ENCODERS.
//
// Design authority: design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §2.3
// (R52.M3, step 2: "#1693 -- BITMAP_SET / BITMAP_CLEAR WAL record
// encoders (op codes 4 and 5 per §2.4). Encoders live in the allocator
// module until the journal module lands at M5; then the encoders
// relocate to journal_ondisk.pdx. This deliberate double-move is
// cheaper than a forward-declaring shim."); on-disk JBNL record layout
// per design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.4 ("Journal region --
// write-ahead log ring").
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scope
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Lands the two record encoders this issue names:
//
//   alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_set(txn_id: u64, bit_no: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> u64
//   alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_clear(txn_id: u64, bit_no: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> u64
//
// Each writes one 24-byte on-disk JBNL record (§2.4's `JOP_BITMAP_SET`
// (op 4) / `JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR` (op 5), payload `{bit_no:u64}`) into the
// caller-supplied `out_ptr`, and returns the record's total length in
// bytes (`WAL_BITMAP_RECORD_LEN` = 24) so a caller staging several
// records back-to-back inside a JBNL block can advance its own cursor
// by the return value. These are consumed by R52.M5's journal replay
// walker (`journal_ondisk.pdx` / `journal_replay.pdx`, #1704/#1705),
// which is also where `kind_pdxfs_txn.pdx`'s `PXT_OP_UNLINK` handler
// (#1711, R52.M6) will call them to emit `JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR` for every
// freed block and `PXT_OP_CREATE` (#1709) will call the SET encoder
// for a freshly allocated inode-table block.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Design-doc gaps flagged (see final report to main / softarch)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. Arity vs. the plan's illustrative sketch. §5.1's arity-hazard
//    table sketches "BITMAP_SET encoder takes (txn_id, bit_no,
//    journal_head_lba, journal_head_off) = 4 for the encoder itself.
//    Fine." That sketch threads two raw integers a caller would use to
//    locate where in the (not-yet-landed) on-disk journal ring to
//    write. No journal-ring module exists yet at M3 (`journal_ondisk.pdx`
//    is #1704, R52.M5) -- there is no `_journal_head[vol_slot]` table,
//    no resident journal-block buffer, and no "journal writer ctx" row
//    for this module to address. Rather than invent that state early
//    (and have M5 either keep it or discard it), this landing collapses
//    `journal_head_lba`/`journal_head_off` into a single `out_ptr`
//    (arity 3, strictly under the 4 the plan already blessed as "fine").
//    This is the same row-pointer/output-pointer collapse §5.1's own
//    rule of thumb prescribes for every other M5 WAL encoder
//    (`journal_encode_block_write`, `journal_encode_dentry_add`); an
//    output pointer is the simplest instance of that collapse. When
//    #1704 lands the on-disk ring, its journal-writer-ctx helper
//    computes `out_ptr = journal_head_block_ptr + current_record_off`
//    once and passes it straight through -- no reshaping of these two
//    encoders' bodies is needed, only their call sites change.
//
// 2. No new M3 failure-taxonomy codes. All eight M3 band slots
//    (0xFFFFED10..ED17) are already frozen by #1692 (four wired, four
//    reserved for #1694/#1695); this issue's design-doc line item does
//    not name a ninth. The encoders are therefore pure formatting --
//    they trust `bit_no` was already range-validated by their caller
//    (`alloc_block`/`free_block` only ever compute `bit_no` as a bit
//    index already checked against `[0, ALLOC_BITMAP_BITS)`, per
//    allocator.pdx) and never fail. This mirrors `wal_append`'s own
//    "checksum is a placeholder the caller's data feeds verbatim"
//    posture in `wal.pdx`, and the row-pointer-trusting posture
//    `alloc_bit_set`/`alloc_bit_clear` already take over their own
//    `bit_index` argument.
//
// 3. Relocation note. Per the plan's own words, this file's two
//    functions (and its three `pub let` constants) are the ones that
//    physically move into `journal_ondisk.pdx` at R52.M5 (#1704) once
//    that module exists to host all nine `JOP_*` encoders together;
//    this file disappears at that point rather than becoming a
//    permanent thin wrapper. Left as a standalone sibling of
//    `allocator.pdx` (rather than appended into it) precisely so that
//    move is a file deletion + a paste, not a surgical extraction from
//    the middle of a larger module.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// On-disk record layout (design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.4)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Record layout (variable-length, 8-byte aligned):
//
//   +0   record_hdr : u64 LE   op[7:0] | flags[15:8] | payload_len[47:16]
//   +8   txn_id      : u64 LE  KIND_PDXFS_TXN row's txn_id
//   +16  payload     : u8[N]   op-specific
//
// For both `JOP_BITMAP_SET` and `JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR`, payload is a single
// `bit_no : u64` (N = 8), so the total encoded record is 24 bytes:
//
//   +0   record_hdr  = op | (0 << 8) | (8 << 16)     (flags always 0 here;
//                                                      no per-record flag
//                                                      is defined for
//                                                      these two ops yet)
//   +8   txn_id
//   +16  bit_no
//
// `record_hdr`'s packing (`mov r10, imm; shl r10, N; or dst, r10`) is
// the same idiom `blkdev_cap.pdx`'s `blkdev_cap_mint_nvme`/`_mint_ahci`
// already prove for packing a small tag into a shifted high field.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Arity discipline (design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §5.1)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_set(txn_id, bit_no, out_ptr)` = 3.
// `alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_clear(txn_id, bit_no, out_ptr)` = 3. Both far
// under the paideia-as ceiling of 6 (see gap 1 above for why this is 3
// rather than the plan's illustrative 4).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Both functions are leaves (no nested `call`, three qword stores and
// one immediate pack): no prologue push is needed, matching
// `allocator.pdx`'s own leaf posture for `alloc_bit_test/_set/_clear`.
// SysV incoming: rdi = txn_id, rsi = bit_no, rdx = out_ptr.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// mov_b / no-test / label-prefix discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No byte-granularity access -- every store is a whole qword. No `test`
// instruction anywhere (no branch in either function's body at all).
// Both functions are single-basic-block leaves, so no internal labels
// are needed and none are declared.

module AllocWal = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // JOP_* op codes this module encodes (full 9-op set is #1704's scope;
  // design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.4).
  // ============================================================================

  pub let WAL_JOP_BITMAP_SET   : u64 = 4
  pub let WAL_JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR : u64 = 5

  // ============================================================================
  // Record geometry constants.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let WAL_BITMAP_PAYLOAD_LEN : u64 = 8    // bit_no : u64
  pub let WAL_BITMAP_RECORD_LEN  : u64 = 24   // record_hdr(8) + txn_id(8) + payload(8)

  // ============================================================================
  // alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_set(txn_id: u64, bit_no: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   Encodes one JOP_BITMAP_SET record at out_ptr. Always succeeds;
  //   returns WAL_BITMAP_RECORD_LEN (24).
  // ============================================================================
  pub let alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_set : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (txn_id: u64) (bit_no: u64) (out_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M3-002 (#1693). Leaf (no nested call, no push needed). Body: (1) r10=8 (WAL_BITMAP_PAYLOAD_LEN); shl r10,16 (-> payload_len field at bits[47:16]). (2) rax=4 (WAL_JOP_BITMAP_SET, op field at bits[7:0]); or rax,r10 (flags stay 0 -- no flag bit is defined for this op) -> rax = record_hdr. (3) [rdx+0]=rax (record_hdr). (4) [rdx+8]=rdi (txn_id). (5) [rdx+16]=rsi (bit_no). (6) rax=24 (WAL_BITMAP_RECORD_LEN); ret. `shl r10,16` is the same pack idiom blkdev_cap.pdx's blkdev_cap_mint_nvme/_mint_ahci already prove. No test instruction (straight-line, no branch). Reserved labels: none needed.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 8;
        shl r10, 16;
        mov rax, 4;
        or rax, r10;
        mov [rdx + 0], rax;
        mov [rdx + 8], rdi;
        mov [rdx + 16], rsi;
        mov rax, 24;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_clear(txn_id: u64, bit_no: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   Encodes one JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR record at out_ptr. Always succeeds;
  //   returns WAL_BITMAP_RECORD_LEN (24).
  // ============================================================================
  pub let alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_clear : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (txn_id: u64) (bit_no: u64) (out_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M3-002 (#1693). Leaf. Identical shape to alloc_wal_encode_bitmap_set except op=5 (WAL_JOP_BITMAP_CLEAR) in place of op=4. Body: (1) r10=8; shl r10,16. (2) rax=5; or rax,r10 -> record_hdr. (3) [rdx+0]=rax. (4) [rdx+8]=rdi (txn_id). (5) [rdx+16]=rsi (bit_no). (6) rax=24; ret. No test instruction. Reserved labels: none needed.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 8;
        shl r10, 16;
        mov rax, 5;
        or rax, r10;
        mov [rdx + 0], rax;
        mov [rdx + 8], rdi;
        mov [rdx + 16], rsi;
        mov rax, 24;
        ret
      }
    }
}
